# Skeleton — <thread-slug> v<N>

<!--
skeleton.md is the memo's communicative INTENT, authored BEFORE any body prose
and expanded INTO the body (issue #752). It is a recursive claim tree that a
reader (or the operator, in one minute) can approve or correct before 3,000
words calcify around it.

Authoring contract (see commands/memo-draft.md §"Author the skeleton before the
body" and rubric.md §"Summary-detail consistency" §"Skeleton↔section derivation
leg"):

  1. ROOT CLAIM — exactly ONE sentence stating what a reader must take away.
     Not a topic ("the economics"), a CLAIM ("the economics force a $457–$1,314
     enterprise price and today it sits $14 above the kill line").
  2. SECTION CLAIMS — one sentence per planned body section. Each is a CLAIM,
     not a topic. Sub-claims may nest under a section claim (the tree is
     recursive) when a section carries more than one load-bearing assertion.
  3. PLAIN-LANGUAGE CONSTRAINT — no term may appear in the skeleton that a
     domain-literate outsider could not parse, UNLESS the skeleton itself
     defines it inline. This forces the jargon question to be settled before
     the body is written. A coinage ("harness-native efficiency") that is not
     defined in the skeleton is a skeleton defect, not a prose defect.

The body is an EXPANSION of the approved skeleton: every body section must
remain answerable to its skeleton claim, and the whole body must deliver the
root claim. The reviewer back-checks skeleton→body derivation at review time
(same MATCH / DIVERGENT / ABSENT / CONTRADICTED verdict tags as the
summary↔detail leg).

Delete this comment block when authoring a real skeleton.
-->

